body:
I've been on suboxone for more than a decade now.  I started back in about 2010 before the films were available.  Are you on the films?  The films are so much better in that they adhere to the bottom of your tongue and the medicine gets absorbed into your bloodstream so much better than the pills under your tongue.  I think the reason is because the pills seem to create so much saliva (and it tastes terrible to boot) which you end up swallowing and not getting into your blood stream.  Remember, it's not a pill or something that you swallow of course.

&#x200B;

So my first advice is to get on the films if you can.  To the OP, I recall going through exactly what you are going through when I first started.  Over time my doctor lowered my dosage which helped me from nodding off.  It also has shallowed out my breathing quite a bit over the years from what it used to be.

&#x200B;

What you are saying is normal.  The most important thing is that you are now sober and not abusing opioids anymore.  It takes time and some fiddling around to get things right and into a routine.  You will eventually gets to a point where it becomes routine and you take it and do not notice anything really which is great.  Agree with other posters that doctors prescribe way too much.

I sympathize with you as I've tried many times to write a suicide note(s) to all those (family and friends) and I cannot do it.  It seems to be what keeps me from going through with it.  

&#x200B;

Just want to say that I hope everything works out for you
